<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Brush My Teeth.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of phrases with names and dates in a short, simple recording delivered at a slow pace. Interactive: Perform tasks based on oral instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of familiar bedtime phrases with names and times from recordings (e.g., "It is time for bed!"). Can perform bedtime tasks from oral instructions (e.g., "It is time to brush your teeth."). Can get the main points in short conversations about bedtime (e.g., "It is time to sleep.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large, colorful picture flashcards of common evening routine items (e.g., a toothbrush, pajamas, a bed, a book). The teacher points to each card and asks simple questions like, "What is this?" or "What do you do with this at night?" (Expecting students to point or give one-word answers, e.g., "Toothbrush!"). The teacher introduces the theme "My Evening Routine" and repeats "First, I brush my teeth." while miming the action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the target vocabulary and actions. For example, the teacher says "brush my teeth" while making a brushing motion, "wear pajamas" while pretending to put on pajamas, and "go to bed" with a sleeping gesture. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher blends sounds for /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "wash" and /t-iː-θ/ in "teeth" by slowly pronouncing the sounds and then saying the full word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple concept checking questions and examples suitable for 3rd graders.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'brush teeth' for morning or for night?" (Expected answer: "Night!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we say 'go to school' or 'go to bed' when we are sleepy at night?" (Expected answer: "Go to bed!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio recording of a child describing their evening routine (approximately 30-45 seconds, slow pace). The recording follows this script: "Hello! My name is Mia. First, I wash my face. Then, I brush my teeth. Next, I wear my pajamas. Last, I go to my bed. I am sleepy. Goodnight!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of familiar bedtime phrases with names and times from recordings (e.g., "It is time for bed!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students engage in listening tasks to grasp the main points and some details.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the recording. Students listen and point to the corresponding picture flashcards or a large poster illustrating Mia's routine as each action is mentioned (e.g., point to "wash face," then "brush teeth").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Circle (Gist):</b> Students receive a simple worksheet with 4-5 pictures of various activities, some from Mia's routine and some not (e.g., brush teeth, eat breakfast, wear pajamas, play soccer, go to bed). They listen to the recording again and circle only the activities Mia talks about in her evening routine.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen for Name:</b> After listening, the teacher asks, "What is the girl's name?" to check for understanding of specific details.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening comprehension of sequencing words ("First, then, next, last"), present simple for daily routines (e.g., "I brush my teeth."), and core vocabulary (bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, brush, wash, sleep, sleepy). Reinforcing pronunciation of /b/ and /t/, and blending /w-ɒ-ʃ/ and /t-iː-θ/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Act (TPR):</b> The teacher calls out single actions or short phrases from the evening routine (e.g., "Wash your face!", "Brush your teeth!", "Wear your pajamas!"). Students listen and perform the corresponding action using Total Physical Response (TPR). The teacher then introduces the sequencing words orally: "First, wash your face. Then, brush your teeth." Students listen and follow the two-step instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Order:</b> Students work individually with a set of four picture cards (wash face, brush teeth, wear pajamas, go to bed) placed randomly on their desks. The teacher plays the audio recording of Mia's routine again. Students listen carefully and arrange their picture cards in the correct sequence as they hear Mia describe her actions, paying attention to "First, then, next, last."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: Listen and Verify:</b> In pairs, Student A describes their *own* very simple evening routine using 2-3 steps and sequencing words (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth. Then I go to bed."). Student B listens attentively and points to the corresponding pictures on their worksheet in the order they hear them. Student B then verifies by asking, "Did you say 'brush teeth' first?" or "Did you say 'go to bed' last?" to confirm their listening comprehension.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Do My Routine!" - A guided listening and physical acting-out activity where students demonstrate comprehension by performing actions based on detailed oral instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> The teacher plays a slightly longer audio recording or reads a descriptive narrative of an evening routine. This recording incorporates more descriptive adjectives and politeness phrases, directly targeting the learning outcome for listening comprehension. (e.g., "Hello, children! It's time for bed. Listen carefully. <b>First</b>, you need to <b>brush</b> your <b>clean teeth</b>. Use your <b>toothbrush</b>! <b>Then</b>, put on your <b>comfortable pajamas</b>. <b>Next</b>, ask your parents, 'Can you read me a <b>bedtime story</b>, please?' <b>Last</b>, go to your <b>dark bed</b> with your <b>pillow</b> and <b>blanket</b>. Close your eyes. You are very <b>sleepy</b>. <b>Goodnight</b>, sleep well!"). As students listen, they physically act out each step of the routine simultaneously and silently, demonstrating their understanding of the instructions, sequencing words, and new vocabulary. Props (e.g., a pretend toothbrush, a blanket) can be encouraged.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students' ability to listen attentively to a multi-step oral routine, process the information including sequencing and descriptive vocabulary, and perform the actions accurately and in the correct order. The focus is on demonstrating comprehensive listening skills rather than speaking fluency at this stage.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during the "Listen and Do My Routine!" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives a final set of 3-4 oral instructions for an evening routine, using some variations from the previous audio. (e.g., "Okay class, listen and do: 1. First, find your pajamas. 2. Then, pretend to brush your teeth. 3. Next, yawn because you are sleepy. 4. Last, lie down on your imaginary bed."). Students perform the actions as instructed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listens attentively to all instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Performs actions in the correct sequence.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates clear understanding of core vocabulary (e.g., pajamas, brush teeth, sleepy, bed).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of sequencing words (first, then, next, last) by ordering actions correctly.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a green card and a red card. The teacher reads two statements, and students raise the green card if they agree/understand, or the red card if they don't or need more help.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand many words about evening routines like 'brush teeth' and 'sleepy'."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow instructions when the teacher tells me what to do at night."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word you heard today about evening time?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to Mia's routine was easy or difficult for you today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> "Did students successfully identify the main actions and sequencing words in the audio? Was the pace of the recordings appropriate for A1.1 3rd graders? Were the new vocabulary words (bed, pajamas, teeth, brush, wash, sleepy) effectively introduced and understood through listening activities? What specific pronunciation challenges were observed with /b/ and /t/, and did the blending practice for /w-ɒ-ʃ/ and /t-iː-θ/ help students' comprehension?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Adjustments and scaffolds for next lesson:</b> "I noticed some students struggled with identifying 'next' versus 'last' in the sequence; I will incorporate more explicit visual cues for sequencing. More TPR for less familiar verbs might be beneficial. I will ensure a consistent and slightly slower pace for future audio inputs if needed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Next lesson, we will continue with 'My Evening Routine'. We will read a short, simple story about a child's bedtime using many of the same vocabulary words we heard and practiced today, focusing on reading comprehension of the routine."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>